===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|philosophy}} To deny the existence or significance of something obviously real or important.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1993|author=Howard Margolis|title=Paradigms and Barriers: How Habits of Mind Govern Scientific Beliefs|publisher=University of Chicago Press|isbn=9780226505237|page=62
|passage=As with the puzzle of what happens during the combustion of a metal in pure oxygen (the "steel wool" experiment), this result can of course be '''quined'''. Taking the phlogistic view, we could say that the calx requires the same phlogiston content as the metal, so of course the amount of water absorbed must be in accord with that.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1999|author=Denis Fisette|title=Consciousness and Intentionality: Models and Modalities of Attribution|publisher=Springer|isbn=9780792359074|page=119
|passage=They deny that mental states and events actually possess the qualitative properties attributed to them by qualia friends and, as a consequence, they advocate '''quining''' qualia.}}
#* '''2000''', Don Ross, ''Introduction: The Dennettian Stance'' in 2000, Don Ross, Andrew Brook and David Thompson, ''Dennett’s Philosophy: A Comprehensive Assessment'', Massachusetts Institute of Technology, [http://books.google.com/books?id=jDwFYqcMEScC&pg=PA14&dq=%22quined%22#v=onepage page 14]:
#*: Qualia are '''quined''' not because Dennett imagines that there is nothing it is like to be conscious, but because no clear demarcation can be drawn between representations of qualitative properties and representations of other sorts of states.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=Nenad Miscevic|chapter='''Quining''' the apriori|title=Knowledge, Language and Logic: Questions for Quine|editors=A. Orenstein; P. Kotatko|publisher=Springer|isbn=9781402002533|page=95}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=W. Martin Davies|title=The Philosophy of Sir William Mitchell (1861-1962): A Mind's Own Place|publisher=Edwin Mellen Press Limited|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=0d_WAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA182&dq=%22quined%22#search_anchor|page=182
|passage=Structure in the phenomenological realm is not something to be “'''quined'''”, but fostered.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=Roy Sorensen|title=A Brief History of the Paradox: Philosophy and the Labyrinths of the Mind: Philosophy and the Labyrinths of the Mind|publisher=Oxford University Press|isbn=9780199728572|page=357
|passage=Daniel Dennett's ''The Philosophical Lexicon'' defines "quine" as a verb: "to deny the existence or significance of something real or significant". Quine has '''quined''' names, intentions, and the distinction between psychology and epistemology. In 1951 Quine '''quined''' the distinction between analytic and synthetic statements.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Daniel Barnett|title=Movement as Meaning: In Experimental Film|publisher=Rodopi|isbn=9789042023857|page=114
|passage=The private language machine and the evolution of a medium: One of the things that Wittgenstein is most famous for is '''quining''' "private language". By saying that private languages can't exist Wittgenstein wanted us to recognize the inescapable function of the social fabric in language's work.}}
#* '''2009''', Andrew Pessin, ''Mental Transparency, Direct Sensaition, and the Unity of the Cartesian Mind'' in 2009, Jon Miller, ''Topics in Early Modern Philosophy of Mind'', Springer, [http://books.google.com/books?id=HDEyqVo_RdAC&pg=PA34&dq=%22quined%22#v=onepage page 34]:
#*: One might object that in this section I’ve not exactly '''quined''' Cartesian qualia, {{...}}
# To [[append]] something to a quotation of itself.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1984|author=Douglas R. Hofstadter|chapter=Analogies and Metaphors to Explain Gödel's Theorem|title=Mathematics: People, Problems, Results|editors=Douglas M. Campbell; John C. Higgins|publisher=Taylor & Francis|isbn=9780534032036|page=274
|passage="Quining" is what I called it in my book. (He certainly didn't call it that!) '''Quining''' is an operation that I define on any string of English. {{...}} Here is an example of a '''quined''' phrase: "is a sentence with no subject" is a sentence with no subject.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1997|author=Nathaniel S. Hellerstein|title=Diamond: A Paradox Logic|publisher=World Scientific|isbn=9789810228507|page=183
|passage=Diamond arises in Gödelian meta-mathematics. In meta-math, sentences can refer to each other's provability, and to '''quining'''. This yields self-reference: T = "is provable when quined" is provable when quined.}}
#* {{quote-newsgroup|en|year=2001|author=Howard Mirowitz|url=https://groups.google.com/d/msg/rec.music.dylan/-glZphUrLHc/ef-3ORmR7oYJ|title=Re: Why is L&amp;T in quotation marks?|newsgroup=rec.music.dylan
|passage=In "Love And Theft", Dylan '''quined''' the love and theft in his songs in the album's title, "Love And Theft". So the subtext, the meaning of the entire album, when preceded by its quotation, its symbol, yields a paradox.}}
#* {{quote-newsgroup|en|year=2001|author=Jim Evans|url=https://groups.google.com/d/msg/rec.humor.oracle.d/RcSlDstxB6M/rTY4DUufepYJ|title=Re: Quining for the fjords|newsgroup=rec.humor.oracle.d
|passage=And, of course, the existence of various sigmonsters guarantees entire '''quined'''-posts.}}

